### Harsh Critic
## Critical Issues

1. **The central “foundation model” claim is not actually established by the evaluation protocol.**  
   **Classification: Structural**  
   The paper’s headline claim is that it is “towards foundation models for MILP” and that “a single model trained on sufficiently diverse MILP problems can effectively generalize to a variety of unseen MILP classes” (Abstract; Introduction; Conclusion). But all core gains are shown in a setup where the training distribution is itself generated by the authors’ LLM-based pipeline from a tiny set of seed classes, and the main held-out test set is just another split of that same synthetic evolutionary process (Sec. 5.2, Table 1). This does not support broad cross-class generalization in the sense the paper suggests; it supports generalization within the family induced by the authors’ generation procedure. The transfer result that matters most for external validity is MIPLIB (Sec. 5.3, Table 3), and there the models are **fine-tuned on MIPLIB train** before testing. That is useful as a pretraining result, but it is not evidence that the pretrained model itself generalizes to unseen real-world classes. So the paper overclaims substantially: it demonstrates that synthetic pretraining helps downstream fine-tuning, not that it has produced anything close to a foundation model for MILP.

2. **The paper’s main empirical advantage is confounded: it does not isolate diversity from scale, data source, and task-label generation quality.**  
   **Classification: Evidential**  
   A central stated insight is that “the diversity of training data has substantially greater impact on model performance than data quantity alone” (Abstract; Introduction; Sec. 5.2, Fig. 4). But the comparison “Ours” vs. Seed / Seed+Param / Seed+VAE changes many things at once: number of classes, total dataset size, source of classes, plausibility/complexity of formulations, and possibly the distribution of labels/examples collected for each task (Sec. 3, Sec. 5.1, Table 1). Figure 4 helps somewhat, but it is not enough to support the broad causal claim because it is presented only for the authors’ generated regime; it does not disentangle whether gains come from more classes, broader parameter ranges, different instance difficulty, or simply far more supervised signal generated by the pipeline. The paper repeatedly interprets these results as evidence for the primacy of diversity, but the current experiments do not isolate that mechanism strongly enough.

3. **The new language–MILP task is too weakly validated to support claims about interpretability or accessibility.**  
   **Classification: Structural**  
   The paper presents “aligning MILP instances with natural language descriptions” as a key contribution and frames it as improving interpretability and accessibility for non-experts (Abstract; Sec. 2.2.3; Introduction). But the actual evaluation is multiple-choice retrieval among 4 or 10 candidate descriptions (Table 1b, Table 2, Table 3), where the descriptions appear tied to class templates produced by the same pipeline that generated the MILPs. That can be solved by class- or template-level matching rather than genuine semantic understanding of an arbitrary MILP instance. Nothing here demonstrates that the system can explain an unseen MILP, recover meaningful semantics beyond class identity, or help a user interpret raw constraints. This is not just “needs a stronger benchmark”: the current metric does not measure the interpretability claim the paper makes.

4. **The branching results are not yet strong enough to support the broader solver-acceleration narrative.**  
   **Classification: Evidential**  
   For learning to branch, the headline framing is acceleration of MILP solving (Abstract; Sec. 2.2.2; Introduction). Yet on the main held-out test set, the only branching comparison in Table 1c is against seed-data variants, not against contemporary learned branching systems in a comparable multiclass setting, and the absolute result is still modest: 70.9% solved with 15.5% time improvement over SCIP default including overhead. This is encouraging, but much weaker than the paper’s broader rhetoric about a foundation-model approach to solver enhancement. Moreover, no variance or significance is reported for these runtime metrics, where heterogeneity across instances is typically large (Sec. 5.1, Table 1c). The evidence supports “pretraining on broader synthetic classes can help a branching policy,” not the stronger practical claim.

## Section-by-Section Notes

### Abstract / Introduction
- The abstract overstates what is shown. “Generalize across problem classes” and “significant improvements on unseen problems, including MIPLIB benchmarks” blur the distinction between **held-out synthetic classes from the same generator** and **fine-tuned transfer to MIPLIB**. The latter is not zero-shot or direct out-of-domain generalization.
- The phrase “foundation model” is used aggressively throughout, but the paper trains **separate models per task** (Sec. 7 acknowledges this). So even on the authors’ own setup, this is much closer to **synthetic multiclass pretraining** than to a foundation model in the usual sense.

### Section 2: Formal Descriptions and Tasks
- In Sec. 2.2.1, the paper motivates integrality gap prediction partly as a predictor of solve time/difficulty. That may be directionally true, but the paper never actually validates this connection empirically. So the practical importance of the task, as framed here, is asserted rather than shown.
- For the language-MILP task (Sec. 2.2.3), the formulation is reasonable as contrastive pretraining, but the paper then makes much stronger claims about interpretability/accessibility than this objective naturally supports.

### Section 3: MILP-Evolve
- This section is the paper’s most interesting component, but key aspects of the generated data distribution remain opaque in the main text. The paper claims “more than a thousand” classes and “rich diversity,” yet the evidence shown in the body is mainly a t-SNE of code embeddings (Fig. 1b). That is not strong evidence that the generated optimization problems are substantively diverse in mathematically meaningful ways.
- The filtering/parameter search step appears powerful enough to shape the resulting instance distribution substantially, but the main text does not tell the reader how often generations are rejected, how feasibility/difficulty are balanced, or whether the same filters are applied comparably to baselines. Since this pipeline defines the benchmark itself, these details matter.

### Section 4: Training Pipeline
- The architecture seems sensible and not overclaimed at the model level.  
- However, for the language task, using NV-Embed-v1 as the text encoder while the MILP side is learned may make the task largely about mapping generated classes onto high-quality pretrained text embeddings of recurring templates. That is not necessarily bad, but it reinforces that the task may mostly measure class/template recognition rather than deep alignment.

### Section 5.1: Experimental Setup
- The baseline set is limited. The paper compares mainly to variants of training data construction on top of similar GNN-style learners, but the claims in the introduction are broader than that. Especially for branching, the paper should be clearer that it is not demonstrating superiority over the best current multiclass or transfer-oriented branching systems, only over the selected seed-data baselines.
- No confidence intervals / standard deviations are reported in the main tables for correlation, retrieval accuracy, or especially branching runtime metrics.

### Section 5.2: MILP-Evolve Test Sets
- The 7:1:2 split over generated classes is appropriate as an internal test, but it cannot bear the interpretive weight the paper puts on it.
- Table 2 is more convincing than Table 1 in spirit because it uses six unseen seed classes to generate new data, but it is still another evaluation inside the authors’ synthetic generation framework, not an external benchmark of broad MILP diversity.
- Figure 4 is suggestive, but the conclusion that class diversity matters more than quantity is stronger than what the figure alone supports.

### Section 5.3: MIPLIB Transfer Learning
- This is the most important external result in the paper. The improvements over scratch/seed pretraining are real and meaningful.  
- But because all reported MIPLIB results involve fine-tuning on MIPLIB train, the section should be interpreted as a **pretraining study**, not as evidence of broad out-of-domain generalization of a pretrained MILP model.
- Omitting branching on MIPLIB is understandable, but it also means one of the three marquee tasks lacks external validation entirely.

### Conclusion
- The conclusion again overstates the paper’s scope. What is supported is a useful synthetic data generation pipeline and evidence that pretraining on it helps multiple MILP tasks. What is not supported is the stronger “foundation model for MILP” framing.

## Strengths

- The paper tackles a genuinely important bottleneck in ML-for-MILP: lack of broad training data across problem classes.
- The idea of **LLM-driven class generation** rather than only instance augmentation is novel and potentially impactful.
- Evaluating the same data-generation idea across three quite different tasks is valuable; it shows the approach is not narrowly tuned to one benchmark.
- The MIPLIB transfer results (Table 3) are the strongest part of the paper and do support the claim that synthetic multiclass pretraining can improve downstream performance.
- The scaling study in Figure 4, while not definitive, is a useful attempt to probe what aspects of the generated data matter.

## Overall Assessment

This paper contains an interesting and potentially important idea—using LLMs to generate diverse MILP classes for synthetic pretraining—and the MIPLIB transfer results suggest that this idea has real promise. But the paper’s central framing is substantially overstated. It does **not** establish a foundation model for MILP, nor does it convincingly show broad generalization across real unseen problem classes; most core evidence comes from splits of the authors’ own synthetic generation process, and the external evaluation is fine-tuned transfer rather than direct generalization. In addition, the interpretability claim built on the language-alignment task is not supported by the chosen metric. I would judge this as a promising synthetic pretraining/data-generation paper with useful empirical results, but the core headline claims do not hold up in their current form, so I would not accept it as is.
### Neutral Reviewer
## Balanced Review

### Summary
This paper proposes a foundation model training approach for Mixed Integer Linear Programming (MILP), where a single deep learning model trained on diverse MILP problem classes generalizes across unseen classes. To overcome the lack of diverse training data, the authors introduce MILP-Evolve, an LLM-based evolutionary pipeline that generates over a thousand diverse MILP classes from seed examples. The framework is evaluated on three tasks—integrality gap prediction, learning to branch, and language-MILP contrastive alignment—showing consistent improvements over baselines on held-out test sets and MIPLIB transfer learning.

### Strengths
1. **Addresses a real and important problem**: The lack of generalization of ML methods for MILP across problem classes is a well-known limitation. The paper convincingly argues that per-class models fail to generalize (e.g., citing Huang et al., 2024), motivating the foundation model approach.
2. **Creative data generation pipeline**: MILP-Evolve uses LLMs with domain-specific evolutionary operators (add constraints, crossover, mutate, delete, create new) to generate diverse MILP classes, which is a novel and practical approach to a hard data scarcity problem. The t-SNE visualization (Figure 1b) qualitatively demonstrates the diversity achieved.
3. **Strong empirical results across multiple tasks**: On the MILP-Evolve held-out test set (Table 1), "Ours" achieves 5.8× correlation improvement for IG prediction, ~1.42× improvement in instances solved for branching, and ~1.92× accuracy improvement for language alignment over Seed baselines. Transfer learning on MIPLIB (Table 3) also shows consistent gains.
4. **Important insight on diversity vs. quantity**: Figure 4 demonstrates that scaling training classes matters far more than scaling instances per class—a valuable finding for the community. Reducing classes while maintaining total instances degrades performance substantially, whereas reducing instances per class has minimal effect.
5. **Novel learning task**: The language-MILP contrastive learning task is original and practically motivated—making MILP instances interpretable by aligning them with natural language descriptions. The 4-way and 10-way accuracy metrics provide clear evaluation.
6. **Comprehensive baselines**: Comparisons against Seed, Seed+Param, Seed+VAE, and an architecture ablation (Ours-Attn) cover both data augmentation and model design alternatives, strengthening the conclusions.

### Weaknesses
1. **"Foundation model" claim is overstated**: The paper trains *separate* models for each of the three tasks, not a single unified model. The conclusion acknowledges this ("we acknowledge that this work still trains separate models for each learning task"), but the title and framing throughout the paper imply a single foundation model. This mismatch weakens the conceptual contribution.
2. **Missing direct comparison with concurrent multi-class work**: The paper cites Huang et al. (2024) as a concurrent work that trains a joint model on five classes, but never directly compares against it. Given that this is the most closely related approach, the absence of this comparison is a significant gap.
3. **Learning-to-branch task incomplete on MIPLIB**: The paper omits branching results on MIPLIB because only 13 instances fall in the suitable solve-time range. This limits the strongest claim (generalization to real-world benchmarks) for the most impactful task.
4. **Limited transparency on MILP-Evolve pipeline details**: Key statistics are missing—what fraction of LLM-generated classes pass the filtering stage? How many of the >1000 classes are genuinely structurally distinct versus minor parameter variations of similar formulations? What is the generation cost? Without these details, reproducibility and understanding of the pipeline's effectiveness are reduced.
5. **Integrality gap metric concerns**: Deviation (MAE relative to |z_LP^0|) can be misleading for instances with small LP relaxation values, potentially inflating error rates. The paper does not discuss this sensitivity or provide alternative metrics.
6. **Attention mechanism lacks theoretical justification**: The attention module over sub-sampled nodes is introduced empirically with limited analysis of why it helps or what global dependencies it captures. The ablation shows it matters, but the mechanism deserves deeper explanation.

### Novelty & Significance
The MILP-Evolve pipeline and the language-MILP contrastive learning task are genuinely novel contributions. The foundation model framing is conceptually important for the MILP+ML community, though the current execution (separate task-specific models) doesn't fully deliver on this vision. The diversity-over-quantity finding is significant and will likely influence future work. Overall, novelty is moderate-to-high, and significance is high given the practical importance of MILP generalization.

### Suggestions for Improvement
1. **Include direct comparison with Huang et al. (2024)**: Train their multi-class approach on the same data and evaluate on the same test sets. This is the most important missing comparison for establishing the contribution.
2. **Report MILP-Evolve pipeline statistics**: Provide pass rates at each filtering stage, a quantitative diversity analysis (e.g., structural feature distributions across generated classes), and estimated generation costs. This strengthens reproducibility and the diversity claim.
3. **Moderate the "foundation model" framing**: Either explicitly position this as "towards" a foundation model with clear limitations stated upfront, or pursue a multi-task unified model to better justify the title.
4. **Report branching results on MIPLIB**: Even with only 13 suitable instances, reporting results (with appropriate caveats) would strengthen the generalization claim for the task with the most practical impact.
5. **Analyze failure modes**: Identify which types of MILP structures or problem classes the model struggles with on the held-out test set, and discuss what structural properties enable successful transfer.
6. **Provide deeper analysis of the attention mechanism**: Explain what global information the attention captures that GCN alone misses, possibly through attention weight visualization or probing experiments.
### Spark
## How to Improve This Paper

### Missing Experiments (top 3-5 only)
1. **Multi-task unified model experiment.** The paper frames this as a "foundation model" approach, but trains three separate models for three tasks. Without at least one experiment showing a single model handling multiple tasks jointly, the "foundation model" claim is unsupported—what exists is just multi-class (not multi-task) learning.

2. **Comparison with Huang et al. (2024).** The paper explicitly identifies this concurrent work as the most related prior (joint training on 5 classes) but never compares against it. This is the most natural baseline for the generalization claim and its absence is conspicuous.

3. **Stronger baselines for integrality gap prediction.** The "Mean" baseline is trivial, and no traditional ML baseline (e.g., random forest on handcrafted MILP features) is included. Without this, it is unclear whether the GNN architecture or just the diverse data drives improvements.

4. **MIPLIB evaluation for learning-to-branch.** The most practically important task is omitted on the key real-world benchmark (only 13 qualifying instances). This severely weakens the generalization-to-real-world claim for the task where it matters most.

### Deeper Analysis Needed (top 3-5 only)
1. **MILP-Evolve data quality statistics.** The paper claims 1000+ classes but never reports how many generated classes passed filtering, rejection rates, or what fraction of "classes" are trivially simple/degenerate. Without this, the diversity claim is unquantified—1000 classes could include many near-duplicates or trivially solvable problems.

2. **Failure mode analysis.** No analysis of which unseen classes the model fails on or why. If the foundation model approach is viable, understanding its limits (e.g., does it fail on classes with very different constraint structures?) is essential for assessing believability.

3. **Statistical significance and confidence intervals.** No error bars, standard deviations, or significance tests are reported for any result. Given the inherent variance in MILP solving and the relatively small test sets (e.g., 13 MIPLIB instances), improvements may be within noise.

4. **Distribution shift analysis between MILP-Evolve and real-world MILP.** The paper claims real-world generalization but never analyzes how the distribution of generated classes (in terms of constraint types, variable ratios, density, etc.) compares to MIPLIB or other real benchmarks. Transfer learning gains could be superficial if generated classes happen to overlap structurally.

### Visualizations & Case Studies
1. **Concrete generated class examples with instance properties.** The auction evolution chain (Fig 3) shows names but not actual constraint matrices, sizes, or difficulty metrics. Showing what the generated classes look like structurally would reveal whether they are meaningfully diverse or superficially different.

2. **Attention map visualization on unseen classes.** The paper claims attention helps transfer (Sec 5.3), but never shows what attention actually captures. Visualizing attention patterns on MIPLIB instances would reveal whether the model learns meaningful global structure.

3. **Per-class performance heatmap on held-out test.** Showing which held-out classes the model succeeds/fails on (organized by structural properties) would expose whether generalization is broad or limited to classes similar to training.

### Obvious Next Steps
1. **Train and evaluate a single multi-task model** on at least two of the three tasks to substantiate the "foundation model" framing.

2. **Expand MIPLIB evaluation** by relaxing the filtering criteria or using additional real-world benchmark sets (e.g., other MIPLIB versions, industrial benchmarks) to provide stronger evidence of real-world generalization.

3. **Compare directly with Huang et al. (2024)** on the same problem classes they use, since this is the most direct competitor for multi-class MILP learning.

### Other stuff you can add
1. The language-MILP contrastive learning task's practical value is asserted but never validated—add a human evaluation or downstream task (e.g., retrieval, formulation editing) to demonstrate that high 10-way accuracy translates to actual interpretability gains.
2. Report compute costs for MILP-Evolve generation (LLM API calls, solver time for filtering) so readers can assess scalability of the data generation pipeline.
### Human Finder
Now I have gathered sufficient information from relevant human reviews. Let me compile the review.

**Strengths:**

1. **Novel and ambitious framing**: The paper is the first to propose a foundation model training approach for MILP learning, training a single model across diverse problem classes rather than class-specific models. As noted in reviews of similar works (Foundation Models for Boolean Logic), "the goal of training a 'foundation model' (or rather: encoder) of [combinatorial] formulas for different downstream tasks is novel and could be useful" [qeY25DwmKO, Reviewer 1].

2. **MILP-Evolve data generation pipeline**: The LLM-based evolutionary framework generating 1000+ diverse MILP classes is a significant contribution to address the data scarcity problem. The t-SNE visualization (Figure 1b) nicely demonstrates the diversity coverage.

3. **Comprehensive multi-task evaluation**: Evaluating across three distinct tasks (integrality gap prediction, learning to branch, language-MILP contrastive learning) tests different facets of MILP understanding, and the consistent improvements across tasks strengthen the empirical case.

4. **Diversity vs. quantity analysis**: The scaling experiments (Figure 4) showing that training class diversity matters more than instance quantity is a valuable insight, consistent with findings in synthetic data literature [oqsQbn4XfT].

**Weaknesses:**

1. **Quality and validity of LLM-generated MILP data is insufficiently verified.** The MILP-Evolve pipeline relies on GPT-4o to generate MILP problem classes via evolutionary prompting, with filtering limited to syntax checks and parameter search. However, as noted in reviews of similar synthetic data generation papers, "using the same model to verify its own reasoning chain and clustering judgements may lead to a positive bias; the verification module would benefit from utilizing [an independent verification]" [oqsQbn4XfT, Reviewer 2]. The paper does not report any human evaluation or expert verification of the mathematical correctness of the generated problem classes, and as DIG-MILP reviewers noted, subtle feasibility or boundedness issues in generated instances could undermine downstream learning [psDvcWtFdE, Reviewer 3: "The relationship between the theoretical derivations and the proposed [generation] pipeline is unclear"]. Without rigorous validation that the generated MILP classes are mathematically sound and non-degenerate, the reliability of the entire framework rests on unverified LLM output.

2. **Limited evaluation on truly out-of-distribution, large-scale, or heterogeneous benchmarks.** The MIPLIB transfer results are encouraging but incomplete: the learning-to-branch task is entirely omitted on MIPLIB because only 13 instances had suitable solve times, which the paper acknowledges. Foundation model reviewers for similarly scoped works have highlighted this concern: "The authors do not perform any out-of-distribution analysis, such as different problem sizes or distributions" [qeY25DwmKO, Reviewer 1], and "the experiments are conducted on relatively small problem instances, which limits the scope of the evaluation" [XTxdDEFR6D, Reviewer 3]. The held-out MILP-Evolve test set may still share structural biases with the training set since they come from the same generation pipeline, making it a weak test of out-of-distribution generalization. The paper would benefit from evaluation on larger-scale instances and on benchmarks beyond MIPLIB.

3. **The paper trains separate models per task, which undermines the "foundation model" claim.** Although the paper acknowledges this limitation in the conclusion, calling this a "foundation model approach" is overclaiming when each task uses a separate model with task-specific architectures (e.g., no attention for branching). As GOAL reviewers noted for a generalist CO model, there is "still a significant gap between [the generalist] and the optimal solutions... Such a gap may also suggest that the time of 'general solvers' has not yet arrived" [z2z9suDRjw, Reviewer 1]. A true foundation model would share parameters across tasks and demonstrate emergent transfer, which this work does not show.

4. **Modest and inconsistent improvements on the learning-to-branch task.** The branching task shows the weakest gains (1.42x improvement in instances solved to optimal), and Table 1c shows that the Seed+Param baseline already achieves 64.52% vs. the model's 70.90%. As CAMBranch reviewers noted, "the improvement is not significant compared with GCNN baseline on many medium and hard datasets" [K6kt50zAiG, Reviewer 3], and branching policies face "generalizability and robustness concerns: the diversity of MILP problems poses a significant challenge... the model's robustness to adversarial inputs or unusual problem structures remains to be thoroughly evaluated" [K6kt50zAiG, Reviewer 2]. The time improvement with network overhead included is only 15.49%, and without the overhead the attention layer is removed—raising questions about the practical deployment viability.

5. **The language-MILP contrastive learning task has limited practical significance and weak evaluation design.** The task is framed as aligning MILP instances with natural language descriptions via n-way accuracy (4-way and 10-way), but it is unclear how this would scale to the vast space of possible MILP formulations in practice. As LLMOPT reviewers noted, "it is questionable whether LLMs are capable of solving large-scale optimization problems. If their applicability is limited to small-scale problems, the significance of this method is unclear" [9OMvtboTJg, Reviewer 1]. The task setup provides a fixed set of text descriptions to choose from, which is far simpler than open-ended description generation or retrieval, and the paper does not compare against alternative approaches for MILP interpretability beyond GPT-4o.

6. **Potential structural bias in the MILP-Evolve test set.** Since both training and test classes are generated by the same evolutionary pipeline from the same seed classes, the "held-out" test set may not be truly out-of-distribution. The new test set in Table 2 uses six unseen seed classes but still follows the same pipeline. RouteFinder reviewers raised a similar concern: "The zero-shot validation for a mere [one constraint] is not convincing as it is very relevant to constraints in training batch. More constraints in zero-shot validation are supposed to make a more solid work" [du9reSRIo1, Reviewer 3]. Testing on structurally diverse external benchmarks beyond MIPLIB would provide stronger evidence of generalization.

