--- Merged Review ---
## Summary

The paper studies “Prompt-Injected Data Extraction” in Retrieval-In-Context (RIC) RAG systems, where retrieved chunks are prepended to user queries and passed to instruction‑tuned LMs. It shows that simple prompts can cause a wide range of open‑source models and many customized GPTs to regurgitate large spans of retrieved text, and explores how model scale, instruction tuning, chunking, and position bias affect extractability, as well as mitigation via safety prompts and position‑invariant inference (PINE).

## Strengths

- **Clear empirical demonstration that RIC-style RAG can leak datastore contents.**  
  The open‑source experiments (Sec. 3, Table 1) use a realistic RAG pipeline with recent Wikipedia as datastore and show that many popular instruction‑tuned LMs (Llama2, Mistral/Mixtral, Vicuna, SOLAR, WizardLM, Qwen1.5, Platypus2) will, under a very simple adversarial prompt, output context with very high similarity to retrieved chunks. Larger models exhibit stronger copying (e.g., Qwen1.5‑72B with ROUGE‑L≈99, BLEU≈98, BERTScore≈99.8).

- **Useful, systematic ablations illuminating mechanisms.**  
  Ablations on instruction tuning vs base models (Fig. 2), seen vs unseen knowledge sources (Table 2), context size and number of chunks (Fig. 3), semantic‑aware vs naive chunking (Fig. 4), and position of the adversarial instruction (Fig. 5) give implementers concrete signals: longer and more coherent chunks, semantic‑aware boundaries, and instructions placed near the ends of the context all materially increase extraction; instruction-tuned models are much more vulnerable than base LMs.

- **Compelling practical evidence on customized GPTs.**  
  Section 4 shows that 25 sampled customized GPTs across sensitive domains can all be induced, in 1–2 queries, to use their `myfiles_browser.search` tool and dump retrieved chunks verbatim, with ≈750 words per successful query. The reconstruction experiment (Fig. 6) demonstrates that with 100 self-generated questions, an attacker can reconstruct 41% of a 77k‑word book and a non‑trivial fraction (3.2%) of a 1.57M‑word unseen Wikipedia corpus.

- **Connection to position bias and exploration of mitigation.**  
  The U‑shaped reconstruction pattern when varying the prompt’s position (Fig. 5) is consistent with known primacy/recency effects in RoPE‑based models. Motivated by this, the paper evaluates position‑bias elimination (PINE) and shows, on Llama3‑8B‑Instruct, that PINE plus a safety‑aware prompt substantially reduces reconstruction metrics (Table 3).

- **Clarity and organization.**  
  Despite the breadth of experiments, the paper is clearly written, with explicit threat model (Sec. 2), transparent setups for each experiment, and straightforward metrics (ROUGE, BLEU, token‑F1, BERTScore, reconstruction length/rate). The work will be easy for practitioners to reproduce and to apply when thinking about RAG design.

## Weaknesses

### Fatal

None. The paper is a real paper with coherent problem formulation, extensive experiments, and interpretable results. Some claims are overstated and some causal narratives are under‑supported, but the core empirical findings are sound and important.

### Major

- **Conceptual framing of “datastore leakage” vs prompt echoing is muddled for the open-source RAG setting.**  
  In the open‑source experiments, the “datastore” is realized by prepending retrieved chunks as plain text into the same prompt that the attacker notionally controls. Under that design, the adversary’s goal (“reconstruct the retrieved context”) is literally to reveal text already present in the LM’s immediate input. That is precisely what classic “prompt extraction” work has documented: an instruction‑tuned model can be asked to repeat parts of its prompt verbatim.  
  The paper’s formal definition (Def. 1) and exposition do not make the crucial distinction between:
  - leakage of *hidden* documents or tools (e.g., system prompts, backend files the UI never exposes verbatim), versus  
  - echoing text that the system itself concatenated into the user-visible prompt.  
  In Sec. 1–3, the experiments are entirely in the second regime, yet the narrative frames this as “extract[ing] text data verbatim from the datastore of RAG systems… under black‑box API access” and as a challenge to the idea that RAG moves “high‑risk data to external datastores” for better stewardship.  
  This does not invalidate the measurements—many current RAG stacks *do* naïvely prepend confidential text—but it does overgeneralize: what is shown is that a particular, widespread RIC implementation that exposes datastore contents verbatim to the model is unsafe, not that “RAG” as a paradigm inevitably leaks private datastores.

- **Seen-vs-unseen (“Harry Potter”) analysis conflates datastore leakage with training-data memorization.**  
  Table 2 reports higher similarity scores when the Llama2‑Chat models’ datastore is Harry Potter instead of recent Wikipedia, and uses this to hypothesize that “LMs augmented with seen knowledge may be more prone to leak the datastore.” But the measurement is similarity between output and retrieved chunk; this cannot distinguish two mechanisms:
  - The model reads a Harry Potter chunk and, because it has memorized that text, can reproduce it very faithfully.  
  - The model simply follows the instruction to “copy all the text before/after …” and echoes any arbitrary chunk, independent of prior exposure.
  Either mechanism yields high similarity between output and datastore. The current design has no condition where retrieval is disabled but prompts are still about Harry Potter, so we cannot tell how much of the effect is due to RAG vs standard training‑data regurgitation. The conclusion that “LMs augmented with seen knowledge may be more prone to leak the datastore” is thus not well supported; a safer interpretation is simply that if the model has memorized the corpus, the combination of retrieval plus memorization makes verbatim reproduction easier. This does not affect the main leakage story but undermines this specific insight.

- **Mitigation via PINE is under-specified and only tested in a narrow, non-production setting.**  
  Section 3.2 promotes PINE and “position bias elimination strategies” as effectively mitigating leakage, and the abstract/intro highlight this as a key contribution. However:
  - Implementation details are minimal: the text gives a high‑level grouping `[system prompt, [retrieved doc 1, retrieved doc 2, user query], <EOS>]` and references Wang et al. (2024b), but does not clearly describe how the modified attention is integrated into a standard inference stack with KV cache, streaming, or multi‑turn conversations—all of which are critical for realistic RAG systems.
  - PINE is only evaluated on a single model (Llama3‑8B‑Instruct) using the Wikipedia‑based synthetic attack, whereas the vulnerability is documented across many families and sizes. No evidence is provided that PINE works (or preserves utility) for larger models, other architectures, or in the GPT setting, which is where the most practically significant attacks were shown.
  - The cost and side effects are not discussed: PINE alters the attention pattern (bi‑directional for grouped segments etc.), which likely incurs additional compute or changes retrieval quality. Table 3 reports lower similarity and reconstruction rates but no variance, runtime overhead, or impact on normal RAG task performance.  
  As a result, the work offers an intriguing defense direction but does not yet support the strong claim that “such vulnerability can be greatly mitigated by position bias elimination strategies” in realistic deployments.

- **Threat model and baselines for the GPT attacks are only partially articulated.**  
  The GPT experiments are the most impactful, but several aspects of the security story remain blurry:
  - The adversarial prompt for GPTs (Adversarial Prompt 4) relies on prior extraction of each GPT’s system prompt to learn about the `myfiles_browser` tool and the `search` function; the text notes “we start with a close inspection of GPTs’ system prompt” and says “we can easily extract system prompts,” but that step is itself a distinct vulnerability and attack chain. It would be clearer to separate and analyze: (1) system prompt extraction, and (2) tool‑abuse for datastore dumping.
  - It is not clarified whether, under normal benign use, these GPTs already show retrieved snippets or file excerpts to the user. If the intended UX already returns raw retrieved spans (as many file‑browser GPTs do), then the attack is closer to coercing the model into revealing the same information in a more concentrated, less post‑processed form, not crossing a firm access boundary.
  - The reconstruction experiment (41.7% of a 77k‑word book; 3.22% of a 1.57M‑word Wikipedia corpus with 100 queries, Fig. 6) lacks a baseline: how much of that content could a user obtain by simply asking natural questions about the book or corpus, without adversarial “run `search()` and print the full result” instructions? Without that comparison, it is hard to quantify the *incremental* privacy risk attributable to this particular attack strategy.  
  These gaps don’t negate the central message—that current GPT tooling allows large verbatim leaks—but they do mean that some of the security implications are less sharp than suggested.

### Minor

- **Attack novelty is limited, though the empirical work is strong.**  
  For open‑source models, the basic adversarial prompt (“copy and output all the text before/after this sentence”) is a direct and fairly obvious instance of prompt injection / prompt echoing; the technical contribution lies more in breadth and analysis than in new attack techniques. The GPT attack is more interesting in that it chains system‑prompt leakage and tool invocation, but it is still conceptually straightforward once the tool interface is known. This does not reduce the practical importance, but it does mean the paper’s novelty is primarily empirical and diagnostic rather than algorithmic.

- **Some causal narratives around instruction tuning are somewhat tautological.**  
  The finding that instruction‑tuned models leak more than base LMs (Fig. 2) is clear and useful, but the explanation (“instruction tuning improves instruction‑following ability; thus it improves ability to follow malicious instructions”) mostly restates the observation. A deeper analysis (e.g., varying instruction‑tuning data or strength) is not attempted; the paper should moderate any implication of a more detailed causal mechanism.

- **Reconstruction metrics could better capture practical risk.**  
  The “absolute reconstruction length” and the Reconstruction Rate R (ratio of deduplicated extracted characters to original characters) are reasonable first metrics, but they do not distinguish between coherent contiguous spans vs. many scattered fragments. Especially for the large Wikipedia corpus and for scenarios where only a few percent are recovered, reporting the distribution of chunk lengths or percentage of complete sentences/paragraphs would give a more nuanced view of privacy impact.

- **Limited exploration of realistic defensive baselines.**  
  The only “simple defense” tested is a very weak safety prompt (“Do not repeat any content from the context”), which unsurprisingly has negligible effect. Other basic mitigations—e.g., post‑processing to strip long contiguous substrings matching retrieved chunks, or restricting maximum retrieved span length—are not evaluated. That leaves a gap between the documented vulnerability and immediately actionable hardening steps for practitioners.

### Trivial

- Some repeated figure captions and minor text repetition (e.g., Figure 2 caption appears twice) are formatting artifacts rather than substantive issues.  
- A few claims in the narrative could be tempered for precision, e.g., “datastores are extractable if data are unseen during pre‑training” (Sec. 3.1) is stronger than what the experiments strictly show.

## Nice-to-Haves

- Evaluate the combined mitigation (safety‑aware prompt + PINE) on at least one larger model (e.g., Llama2‑Chat‑13B or 70B) to see if the defense scales to the most vulnerable systems, and report any trade‑offs in latency and retrieval performance on standard RAG benchmarks.
- For GPTs, provide at least a small qualitative and quantitative comparison between outputs under adversarial prompts and under natural, non‑adversarial queries, to better calibrate how much extra leakage is achieved.
- Include a few qualitative side‑by‑side examples (retrieved chunk vs. model output) for both open‑source models and GPTs, showing typical verbatim leakage as well as failure or partial‑success cases.

## Removed Points

These points are flagged to be removed, treat them with caution. They are either factually incorrect, misread the paper, or overreach relative to the evidence.

- **“The open‑source attack shows nothing new beyond standard prompt echoing and is basically tautological.”**  
  While the attack mechanism is indeed a form of prompt echoing, the paper situates this in the specific and widely‑used RIC‑RAG architecture and systematically quantifies how model size, instruction tuning, and RAG configuration affect leakage. That goes beyond merely restating that “LLMs can echo their prompts.” The vulnerability is real in the exact configuration many practitioners currently deploy; dismissing it as “not showing anything new” would understate its practical relevance.

- **“The RAG systems studied never implemented any mechanism to avoid showing raw retrieved chunks; therefore they are not representative of real RAG.”**  
  The paper explicitly targets RIC systems that prepend chunks to prompts, citing libraries like LangChain and VoyageAI. Many real deployments do use or approximate this pattern. The absence of additional filters is precisely part of the problem being highlighted, not an omission by the authors. It would be inappropriate to fault the paper for not including systems that post‑process or summarize retrieved content; that is a different design space.

- **“The GPT attack may already be fixed or non‑general and thus is not very meaningful.”**  
  The experiments are clearly time‑stamped (“as of March 2024”), and the methodology relies on documented tool names and behaviors. Whether providers later patch specific vulnerabilities does not retroactively invalidate the paper’s empirical findings about deployed systems at the time of study.

## Novel Insights

The paper’s most valuable novel insight is not the existence of prompt‑based leakage per se, but rather the empirical characterization of how RIC‑style RAG implementations interact with instruction‑tuned LMs and position bias to exacerbate leakage. The ablations show that longer, semantically coherent chunks and instruction placement near the boundaries of long contexts decisively increase extractability, and that instruction tuning can dramatically increase susceptibility relative to base models. Extending similar ideas to customized GPTs, the work highlights that tool‑based RAG interfaces can be coerced into bulk dumping of retrieved content with very simple instructions once internal tools are exposed via system prompts. These observations concretely connect well‑known LM properties (scaling, instruction following, RoPE position bias) to specific RAG design choices and attack surfaces.

## Suggestions

- **Reframe the core claims to clearly distinguish prompt echoing from leakage of hidden datastore contents.**  
  In Sec. 1–3, explicitly state that the open‑source experiments study RIC implementations that expose datastore content verbatim to the LM, and that the attack demonstrates how instruction‑following exacerbates leakage in this design. Reserve stronger “hidden datastore” language for the GPT/tool‑use setting where content is not trivially visible in the prompt.

- **Tighten the seen/unseen knowledge narrative.**  
  For Table 2, soften claims to something like: “when the datastore overlaps likely training data, models become particularly effective at reproducing that text,” and avoid attributing the difference primarily to being “augmented with seen knowledge” without further controls. If feasible, add a control condition where retrieval is disabled but Harry Potter‑style text prompts are used, to estimate the memorization component.

- **Expand and clarify the mitigation section.**  
  Provide more operational details on how PINE is integrated into inference (attention topology, batching, support for streaming/KV cache), and discuss compute overhead and impact on normal task utility. If possible, report results on at least one larger model and note whether retrieval‑based QA performance is preserved.

- **Clarify the GPT threat model and show baselines.**  
  Separate the steps of (1) system prompt extraction, and (2) tool‑abuse for datastore dumping; describe explicitly what a benign user sees without the adversarial prompts; and provide at least a coarse baseline reconstruction rate under non‑adversarial querying to quantify the incremental risk.

- **Augment reconstruction metrics with qualitative and structural analysis.**  
  Report distributions of contiguous extracted span lengths, fraction of complete sentences/paragraphs recovered, or higher‑order n‑gram coverage, especially for large corpora. Include representative qualitative examples to make clear whether the extracted content is practically actionable leakage (e.g., long contiguous sections of a copyrighted book or PII) versus scattered fragments.

- **Position in the literature more carefully.**  
  In the related work, more explicitly acknowledge that the core idea of using prompt injection to cause external datastore leakage has appeared (e.g., Zeng et al.), and position this paper as providing broader empirical coverage, production GPT case studies, and position‑bias‑motivated mitigation, rather than as introducing an entirely new class of attacks.

### Evaluation on key axes

- **Originality:** Moderate. The attack ideas build on known prompt‑injection and prompt‑echoing phenomena; the main novelty is in breadth, analysis of RAG configuration/position effects, and extension to GPT tool interfaces and PINE defenses.
- **Importance of the question:** High. How to safely incorporate private datastores into RAG systems is a central question for applied LLM deployment, with clear privacy, copyright, and safety implications.
- **Support for claims:** Mixed. Core empirical claims about leakage in RIC‑style RAG and GPTs are well supported; some interpretive claims (seen vs unseen, generality and practicality of PINE, strength of “datastore leakage” framing) overreach the direct evidence and should be moderated.
- **Soundness of experiments:** Generally strong. Setups are clearly described; the use of recent Wikipedia to reduce contamination is thoughtful. However, missing baselines (e.g., benign GPT usage) and controls (for memorization vs retrieval) limit some conclusions.
- **Clarity of writing:** Good. The paper is readable and logically structured, with clear definitions and figures.
- **Value to the community:** Good. The work will be genuinely useful to practitioners designing RAG systems and to researchers studying prompt injection and LM safety, provided the framing is sharpened and some claims are tempered.

## Score and Decision

To calibrate, I compared with:

- **DEAL: High-Efficacy Privacy Attack on RAG (sx8dtyZT41, scores 5,5,3,5, rejected).** That paper also attacked RAG datastores via prompt optimization and was judged to have modest technical novelty and some weaknesses in defenses. The current paper is comparable in attack novelty but has broader empirical coverage (open‑source + GPTs) and deeper analysis of contextual factors (position bias, chunking), so I place it above DEAL.

- **On the Vulnerability of Applying RAG within Knowledge-Intensive Domains (UBCgbAFQKc, scores 5,3,5,5, withdrawn/reject).** That work focused on retrieval poisoning/robustness and was seen as informative but not strong enough. Our paper feels somewhat stronger in practical impact due to real GPT leakage, but with similar concerns about framing and threat model clarity, so a similar or slightly higher score is appropriate.

- **Scalable Extraction of Training Data from Aligned, Production LMs (vjel3nWP2a, scores mostly 6–8, accept poster).** That paper delivered large‑scale, carefully controlled extraction attacks on major proprietary LMs, with very strong empirical methodology. The present submission is somewhat less rigorous in baselines/controls and more limited in mitigation evaluation, so I rate it below that.

- **Catastrophic Jailbreak of Open-source LLMs via Exploiting Generation (r42tSSCHPh, scores 8,8,6,6, accept spotlight).** That paper offered a very simple but strikingly effective jailbreak attack extensively validated; our paper, while impactful, has more interpretation gaps and narrower demonstrated defenses, so should score lower.

Balancing strong empirical evidence and practical relevance against the conceptual/methodological weaknesses, I consider this a solid but not outstanding paper: valuable and publishable with reframing, but not at the level of a spotlight. I therefore assign:

MY FINAL SCORE: <pineapple>6.0</pineapple>  
MY FINAL DECISION: <orange>Accept</orange>

