--- Merged Review ---
## Summary
The paper proposes Intra-model Ensemble Learning (IEL), a test-time adaptation procedure for independently pre-trained classifiers. For each unlabeled test input, the method selects the ensemble member with the highest confidence on the majority-voted class as a soft teacher and minimizes cross-entropy from this teacher to all models, updating all weights via backprop. Experiments on CIFAR-10/100-C and ImageNet-C show substantial improvements over static (non-adapted) ensembles on many corruption types, alongside some clear failure modes.

## Strengths
- **Clear and simple mechanism.** The IEL objective is easy to understand and implement: for each input, choose a majority-vote, high-confidence softmax as teacher and align all models to it via cross-entropy. Algorithm 1 and Sec. 3 provide enough detail to reproduce the method (including freezing BN, batch size 1, optimizer setup).
- **Extensive empirical coverage over corruptions and architectures.** The paper evaluates on CIFAR-10-C, CIFAR-100-C, and ImageNet-C, covering all 15 corruptions for CIFAR variants and a wide set for ImageNet-C. It uses heterogeneous ensembles (ResNet, VGG, MobileNet, ShuffleNet, RepVGG, various ResNets), demonstrating that IEL can handle diverse architectures.
- **Substantial improvements on many corruptions.** Tables 1–3 show large accuracy gains on several blur- and artifact-type corruptions, e.g., +20.36% on CIFAR-10-C zoom blur (ResNet20), +23.7% on ImageNet-C glass blur (ResNet50), and consistent ensemble-level gains for many corruptions. Some evaluation-set improvements even exceed tuning-set gains, suggesting nontrivial generalization within the same corruption distribution.
- **Honest reporting of negative results and catastrophic forgetting.** For CIFAR-10/100-C, the authors report not only gains but also clear degradations (e.g., −31% for VGG11 on Gaussian noise in Table 1), and they explicitly discuss catastrophic forgetting and late-epoch degradation (Sec. 3.1, Sec. 4.1, Fig. 3) rather than hiding these issues.
- **Connection to entropy-based TTA.** Fig. 1 empirically shows a positive correlation between IEL loss and Shannon entropy across ImageNet-C corruptions, supporting the claim that IEL indirectly reduces output entropy, which ties it to the broader TTA literature that uses entropy minimization as an unsupervised signal.

## Weaknesses

### Fatal
None of the issues are so severe that this is “not a paper,” but there are structural problems that substantially weaken the claimed contributions and, in my view, justify rejection in its current form rather than revision.

### Major

- **(1) Mismatch between claimed “single-sample TTA” framing and actual experimental protocol.**  
  The paper consistently frames IEL as a method for “single sample test-time adaptation” and emphasizes scenarios where only one sample is available at a time and batch statistics cannot be estimated (Abstract; Sec. 1; Contributions: “single sample setting for TTA”). Algorithm 1 indeed uses batch size 1 and processes one input at a time, but the experimental protocol is much more permissive than what “single-sample TTA” usually denotes:
  - For CIFAR-10/100-C, they use “all 10,000 samples per corruption type for adaptation” (Sec. 4), and apply IEL “for several epochs on the corruption types” (Sec. 4). So each sample is revisited multiple times across epochs.
  - For ImageNet-C, they use a 7,000-sample subset per corruption, with a 90% “tuning set” used for IEL and 10% “evaluation set” from the same corruption distribution (Sec. 4).
  
  Thus, IEL is evaluated in a regime much closer to unsupervised domain adaptation / transductive learning: repeated gradient-based adaptation on thousands of unlabeled target-domain samples over multiple epochs, with a held-out subset from the same distribution for evaluation. The “needs only a single sample” phrasing is literally about batch size, but in combination with the motivating examples (medical, finance, where you might truly get one sample at a time with no revisits) it over-suggests applicability to much harsher online TTA settings than are actually tested. This does not make the experiments invalid, but it significantly narrows the setting for which the results genuinely provide evidence and calls for a more careful, modest framing.

- **(2) Absence of any comparison to standard or even simple TTA / self-training baselines.**  
  Sec. 2.2 positions IEL relative to entropy-minimization TTA methods (TENT, EATA, ROID, CoTTA, etc.), and Sec. 4.1 argues it is an effective TTA approach. Yet the only baselines in Tables 1–3 are:
  - Static individual models (no adaptation),
  - Static ensemble outputs (majority vote, average).
  
  There is no comparison to:
  - Entropy-minimization baselines on the same unlabeled corruption data (e.g., TENT-style adaptation with batch size 1 and frozen BN),
  - Simple self-training or pseudo-labeling where each model adapts by cross-entropy to its own top-1 prediction,
  - Knowledge-distillation style baselines, such as “one strong fixed teacher” supervising all others, or independent adaptation of each model followed by static ensembling.
  
  Given IEL performs full backpropagation into all weights for multiple epochs on thousands of target samples, much of the gain may simply come from generic unsupervised fine-tuning on the target distribution. Without these baseline comparisons, the paper does not establish that its specific intra-ensemble mechanism (majority-vote high-confidence teacher) is superior—or even competitive—to far simpler or more standard adaptation strategies using the same data and similar or lower compute. This undermines the central methodological claim.

- **(3) Conceptual mischaracterization as a “diversity-based optimization signal.”**  
  The first bullet in “Our Contributions” claims: “This work proposes diversity as a new optimization signal for classification in the ensemble learning setting…”. The conclusion similarly refers to “a new diversity-based optimization signal during inference.” However, the loss (Eq. (1)):
  \[
  \mathcal{L} = \frac{1}{M} \sum_{i=1}^M \delta(h_{\theta_i}(\mathbf{x}), H(\mathbf{x})),
  \]
  where \(H(\mathbf{x})\) is the majority-voted, highest-confidence model, explicitly *reduces* diversity by forcing all models to agree with a single teacher distribution. The paper itself notes: “by minimizing it we force members to agree with the majority voted model” (Sec. 2.1) and “we minimize the diversity of the ensemble (we force models to agree with each other)” (Sec. 1–2 discussion). There is:
  - No formal definition or measurement of ensemble diversity,
  - No use of diversity as an explicit *signal* (e.g., adapting more where disagreement is high),
  - No empirical analysis of diversity before vs. after IEL.
  
  In effect, the method uses a pseudo-label / teacher-distribution signal; the ensemble is used to *select* a teacher, not to optimize “diversity” per se. Calling this a “diversity-based optimization signal” is misleading and overstates the conceptual novelty.

- **(4) Lack of evidence that the intra-ensemble mechanism is better than simpler ensemble-based variants.**  
  A key narrative is that models “learn from each other” and that this is qualitatively different from standard ensembles or static knowledge distillation. But the experiments never contrast IEL with:
  - A “fixed teacher” baseline: pick the best pre-trained model on the target corruption and hold it fixed as teacher for all others using the same loss,
  - A “per-sample most confident teacher” without majority-vote constraint,
  - Per-model self-training plus ensembling at test time.
  
  Without such ablations, it is unclear whether:
  - The majority-vote constraint is actually beneficial relative to simply using the most confident model overall or per sample; and
  - The mutual adaptation of teacher and students is helpful or, in some regimes, harmful relative to freezing the teacher.
  
  The current results do show that *some* form of unsupervised adaptation helps on many corruptions, but they do not isolate the contribution of the specific intra-ensemble design.

- **(5) Reporting only “best-epoch” improvements obscures stability and practical usability.**  
  Tables 1–3 report “Highest accuracy improvements (%) over all epochs” per corruption, i.e., the maximum gain at any epoch of IEL training. However:
  - Sec. 3.1 states: “peak average accuracy … is reached at 5 training epochs, with future epochs trending towards the static model baseline. In some experiments we found that the average model accuracy … reduced below the static model accuracy by the final epoch.”
  - Fig. 3 (uncorrupted ImageNet) visually confirms this: accuracy peaks around epoch 4 then drifts down, ending only slightly above static but with periods close to or below it.
  
  In any genuine test-time scenario without labels, you cannot know which epoch would have been optimal. Reporting best-epoch gains therefore acts as an oracle early-stopping metric and can substantially overstate the reliably achievable benefit. There is no reporting of:
  - Performance at a fixed, pre-specified number of passes (e.g., one pass through the tuning set),
  - Any stopping rule based on unlabeled signals (e.g., loss plateau, ensemble agreement),
  - Variability across runs.
  
  Given the acknowledged catastrophic forgetting, this is a serious evidential gap: IEL appears sensitive to over-adaptation, but the presented numbers emphasize optimistic peaks rather than stable performance.

- **(6) Large and systematic failures on several corruption types without mitigation strategy.**  
  The authors candidly report that IEL “induces catastrophic forgetting on only 3 of the 15 corruption types in CIFAR10C … and 4 of the 15 corruption types in CIFAR100C” (Sec. 4.1). The tables show severe degradations on noise-like corruptions: e.g.,
  - CIFAR-10-C Gaussian Noise: VGG11 drops −31.01% on tuning and −27.50% on evaluation; majority vote and most models degrade substantially (Table 1).
  - CIFAR-100-C Gaussian Noise, Shot Noise, Impulse Noise: all ensemble and individual accuracies degrade by roughly 6–20% (Table 2).
  
  The only mitigation discussed is very high-level “future work” (Sec. 4.1 Limitations), such as alternative divergences or weight restoration, but there is no attempt to:
  - Detect when IEL is harming performance,
  - Design confidence, agreement, or loss-based gating to avoid adapting on extremely unreliable predictions,
  - Quantify how often, across corruptions and models, IEL helps vs. hurts at a practical stopping time.
  
  For a method pitched as TTA in high-stakes settings (medicine, safety), these substantial, uncontrolled failure modes are problematic.

### Minor

- **Stationary, per-corruption setting limits realism.**  
  The experiments always stream data from one corruption type at a time, resetting model weights between corruptions (Sec. 4). This is a stationary, single-shift scenario; while it aligns with how CIFAR-C/ImageNet-C are often used, it does not test behavior under non-stationary or mixed shifts (e.g., alternating corruptions, gradually changing conditions), which is where catastrophic forgetting would likely be most severe. The paper cites works discussing non-stationarity (Sec. 2.2, entropy minimization issues) but does not empirically evaluate IEL in those regimes.
  
- **Unclear positioning relative to standard domain adaptation.**  
  Especially for ImageNet-C, where 90% of the target-domain corrupted images per corruption are used over multiple epochs and 10% are held out, the setting is very close to unsupervised domain adaptation with a validation set from the same domain. The TTA framing is plausible for CIFAR-C with full test-set adaptation, but the line between TTA and UDA is blurred; clearer discussion of how the setting relates to standard UDA assumptions would improve clarity.

- **Claims about human collaboration are overstated.**  
  The human-collaboration analogy (Sec. 1–2) and the contribution bullet about “solid step forward in understanding human collaboration and ensemble methods” go beyond what is substantiated. The method is fundamentally a form of mutual distillation; while the analogy is motivational, it should not be presented as an actual contribution to understanding human collaboration.

- **Loss design acknowledged as suboptimal but not explored experimentally.**  
  The authors point out that using cross-entropy with the majority-voted model causes that model’s loss term to equal its entropy (non-zero) and “we risk overwriting the strong knowledge of the majority voted models,” suggesting KL or other 0-at-identity divergences for future work (Sec. 3). This is an important design concern directly tied to catastrophic forgetting, but no small-scale ablations are provided to show its impact.

- **No calibration or reliability analysis.**  
  Given that IEL implicitly reduces entropy and encourages high-confidence predictions (Fig. 1), calibration is a natural concern, especially under distribution shift. The paper does not measure calibration (e.g., ECE) or provide any qualitative analysis of overconfidence, even though related TTA work is cited precisely for such pitfalls.

### Trivial
- Some notation for \(H(\mathbf{x})\) is slightly awkward (mixing arg max over sets of vectors) but the intended definition is clear from context. This does not materially affect the work.

## Nice-to-Haves
- An explicit diversity metric (e.g., average pairwise KL, disagreement rate, correlation of errors) measured before and after IEL across epochs, and correlated with accuracy, would clarify the actual diversity–accuracy tradeoff that IEL induces.
- Experiments on natural distribution shifts (e.g., ImageNet-R, ObjectNet) or more application-driven non-synthetic shifts would strengthen the practical case.
- Ablations over ensemble size (M) and composition (e.g., only two models vs. many; mixing very strong and weak models) to understand how benefit scales with ensemble richness.
- A simple, unlabeled stopping rule (e.g., stop when ensemble agreement exceeds a threshold for most recent samples, or when IEL loss plateaus) plus reporting performance under such a rule.

## Removed Points
These points are flagged to be removed, treat them with caution.

- **“Fundamentally incompatible” with test-time adaptation because of multi-epoch passes on test data.**  
  While the harsh critic describes this as fundamentally incompatible with TTA, the paper explicitly states in Sec. 3 that each sample is seen once per epoch but does not claim strict one-pass online adaptation, and TTA literature does include settings that allow multiple passes over unlabeled test data. The main valid concern (kept above) is the gap between the strict “single-sample, online” narrative and the more permissive experimental regime, not that the work is “not TTA at all.”
- **Implying corruption subsets are cherry-picked on ImageNet-C.**  
  The critic speculates about omitted corruption types, but Table 3 lists a broad set and the text claims “on ImageNetC no catastrophic forgetting was encountered on any of the corruption types (Table 3).” There is no direct evidence in the provided text that the authors selectively excluded unfavorable corruptions from ImageNet-C; the more concrete, supportable issue is the best-epoch reporting and lack of fixed-epoch metrics, which we already include as a major weakness.

## Novel Insights
The paper illustrates, with unusually detailed corruption-wise tables and per-epoch analysis, that strong unsupervised adaptation signals on unlabeled target data can both significantly improve and seriously harm performance in different regimes, even within the same method. In particular, it highlights a striking pattern: a majority-vote, high-confidence ensemble teacher can yield large gains on structured corruptions (blurs, contrast, artifacts) while catastrophically failing on heavy noise corruptions, and that this behavior interacts strongly with over-time adaptation dynamics. This nuanced view—showing both genuine potential and sharp failure modes of mutual distillation among independently trained models under distribution shift—is a useful empirical insight for the TTA and ensemble-learning communities, even if the specific method is not yet ready for deployment.

## Suggestions

- **Reframe the problem setting more precisely.**
  - Clarify that IEL is evaluated in a setting where many unlabeled target-domain samples per corruption are available and may be revisited across epochs; avoid framing it as solving the strict “only one test sample ever available” scenario.
  - Explicitly position the ImageNet-C experiments as closer to unsupervised domain adaptation on a target domain with a validation subset.

- **Add strong baseline comparisons.**
  - Implement at least one entropy-minimization TTA baseline (e.g., TENT-style) adapted to batch size 1 and frozen BN, and a simple self-training baseline (each model adapts on its own predictions) using the same data and comparable training budget.
  - Include a knowledge-distillation baseline where a fixed best teacher supervises the rest, and/or independent adaptation of each model followed by static ensembling.

- **Change the main metrics to reflect realistic usage.**
  - Report performance at fixed passes (e.g., 1 pass, 2 passes) through the unlabeled tuning set, or under a simple unlabeled stopping rule, in addition to best-epoch improvements.
  - Include plots like Fig. 2 for several representative corruptions (including ones where IEL fails) to make degradation patterns transparent.

- **Align claims with the actual loss and evidence.**
  - Remove or soften the “diversity-based optimization signal” framing; instead, describe IEL as a dynamic, ensemble-based pseudo-label / distillation scheme.
  - Tone down the “solid step forward in understanding human collaboration” claim; keep the analogy but present it clearly as illustrative.

- **Address failure modes more concretely.**
  - Analyze why noise corruptions are particularly harmful (e.g., majority vote accuracy, confidence calibration) and explore simple safeguards, such as:
    - Adapting only when ensemble confidence or agreement exceeds a threshold;
    - Weighting the loss by confidence margins;
    - Trying alternative divergences (KL) that reduce updates to the current teacher’s own parameters.

- **Include computational cost analysis.**
  - Provide wall-clock time, FLOPs, or similar metrics for IEL vs. single-model TTA baselines, and discuss when the extra cost of adapting multiple models simultaneously is justified by the observed gains.

## Score and Decision

### Calibration
Given the instructions’ provided anchors (TTA-related works):

- Papers like *Test-Time Ensemble via Linear Mode Connectivity* (4wk2eOKGvh, scores 6–8, accepted) combined novel framing with strong baselines and thorough comparisons.
- Several TTA papers with missing or weak baselines, over-optimistic reporting, or unclear settings received scores around 3–5 and were rejected (e.g., FEATHER, 6yJuDK1DsK, mostly 3–5; others like eXrUdcxfCw, PxL35zAxvT with similar weaknesses in baselines and evaluation rigor also rejected around 3–5).

Relative to these:
- This paper’s empirical effort (breadth of corruptions, models) is solid, and the core idea is intuitive and clearly described.
- However, the lack of any TTA/self-training baselines and the optimistic best-epoch reporting are serious, central issues for a TTA paper.

On that spectrum, this work is clearly stronger than a 1–2 (it is a real, nontrivial paper with extensive experiments), but falls short of the rigor and positioning needed for a 6+. It aligns most closely with rejected TTA papers in the 3–5 range that had interesting ideas but insufficient comparative evaluation.

I therefore assign:

