--- Merged Review ---
## Summary

The paper proposes R-Sparse, a training-free activation sparsity method for transformer LLMs with non-ReLU activations. Each linear layer is decomposed into (i) a sparse path that computes only on large-magnitude input channels and (ii) a low-rank path obtained via SVD of the weight matrix. A per-layer evolutionary search chooses the tradeoff between sparsity and rank. Applied to all linear layers in Llama-2/3 and Mistral, the method reports about 50% “model-level sparsity” with accuracy close to dense models on a suite of QA, LM, and summarization benchmarks, and up to ~40–43% decoding speedup with a custom kernel.

## Strengths

- **Clear, coherent method bridging two known ideas.** The decomposition \(Y = Y_s + Y_r\) with \(Y_s\) using input sparsification and \(Y_r\) using an SVD-based low-rank approximation is clean and well explained (Sec. 3.4, Fig. 4). It directly leverages observed structure in the joint “input channel × SVD component” importance matrix (Sec. 3.3, Fig. 1/3) and avoids output-channel prediction logic that prior activation sparsity methods require.

- **Training-free and applies to modern non-ReLU LLMs across attention and MLP.** The method needs no retraining or continual pre-training, unlike ReLUfication approaches, and is applied systematically to all seven linear layers per block (four attention, three MLP) in Llama-2, Llama-3, and Mistral (Sec. 3.1, 3.4, 4.1).

- **Empirically strong accuracy at nominal 40–50% sparsity across models and tasks.**  
  - Table 1 shows that for all three model families, R-Sparse at 50% model-level sparsity stays quite close to the full model across eight commonsense QA tasks, typically within a few percentage points, and consistently outperforms CATS and GRIFIN at similar or lower nominal sparsity.  
  - Figure 5 extends this to WikiText-2 (perplexity) and XSUM (Rouge-L), where performance remains close to dense up to about 50% sparsity and is often superior to the baselines at equal sparsity.

- **Solid ablations clarifying contributions.**  
  - Table 3 shows that pure activation sparsity (“Sparse”) at 50% is competitive but slightly worse than R-Sparse, and pure low-rank (“Low-Rank”) catastrophically degrades performance. This supports the need for the mixed design.  
  - Table 4 shows that searched per-layer sparse/rank ratios (“Adaptive”) are consistently better than uniform recipes, with up to ~1.6% gains at high sparsity, confirming that layerwise rank-awareness matters.  
  - Table 2 shows compatibility with 4-bit GPTQ quantization, where combining INT4 + R-Sparse still stays reasonably close to the FP16 baseline on several QA tasks.

- **Demonstrated end-to-end speedups, not just FLOP counting.** Section 4.3 and Figure 6 report actual token/s throughput gains (up to ~42–43% at 50% sparsity) for Llama-2-7B and Llama-3-8B, using a custom Triton kernel and realistic prompt/generation lengths.

- **Writing and presentation are clear.** The motivation (Sec. 3.2–3.3), method (Sec. 3.4–3.5), and experiments are generally well-structured and easy to follow, with intuitive visualizations.

## Weaknesses

### Fatal

None. The method is coherent, implemented, and empirically validated; there is no single flaw that invalidates the main empirical claims. However, some structural and evidential issues materially weaken the strength of the conclusions and novelty.

### Major

- **Ambiguity and limited rigor in “model-level sparsity” and fairness of comparisons.**  
  The central claim is “comparable performance at 50% model-level sparsity,” with comparisons to CATS and GRIFIN at “22%/33% model-level sparsity” and scaled-up versions at 40% (Sec. 1, 4.1; Table 1; Fig. 5). However:
  - The paper never gives a precise definition of *model-level sparsity* that maps its mixed activation-sparse + low-rank scheme to an overall compute or memory metric, nor how this number is computed. Sec. 3.4 gives a relative memory I/O formula for a single layer, \(r\frac{m+n}{mn} + s\), but there is no derivation, no mapping from \((r,s)\) per layer to the quoted “40% / 50% model-level sparsity,” and no check that baselines are matched on the same notion of I/O or FLOPs.
  - For CATS and GRIFIN, the paper uses model-level sparsity values “reported” in their work (Sec. 4.1), but those methods (i) sparsify only MLPs, (ii) use different sparsity mechanisms, and likely (iii) rely on slightly different accounting conventions. Here, R-Sparse also sparsifies attention and introduces low-rank factors, yet the accounting is not made explicit.
  - When they “scale up the MLP block sparsity for both methods” to reach higher model-level sparsity (Sec. 4.1), there is no description of how the new sparsity budgets are chosen, whether the baselines are retuned for these higher ratios, or whether effective compute/I/O is actually comparable to R-Sparse at each point in Fig. 5.

  Since the main story is “we achieve similar accuracy at higher model-level sparsity and better than existing methods,” this lack of precise, shared sparsity definition undermines the strength of the comparative claims. The results still show R-Sparse is a strong training-free method, but the current paper does not conclusively demonstrate that it is better than CATS/GRIFIN *at matched effective compute or I/O*.

- **Efficiency evaluation is too narrow to support strong deployment claims.**  
  The abstract and conclusion emphasize “43% end-to-end efficiency improvements,” but the supporting evidence (Sec. 4.3, Fig. 6) is limited:
  - The comparison is only against a dense FP32 HuggingFace baseline on a single A6000 GPU, not against highly optimized dense FP16/BF16 kernels or production inference frameworks. For practical deployment, one would expect FP16/BF16 (or lower) dense baselines, and the comparison to FP32 likely overstates speedups relative to typical setups.
  - No sparse baselines (e.g., CATS, GRIFIN) are benchmarked with their own or any sparse kernels. Thus, we cannot tell whether R-Sparse is competitive in practice against alternative activation sparsity designs.
  - Critical kernel details are omitted: layout of sparse matrices, how indices are computed and reused, how the low-rank path is fused with the sparse path, whether thresholds are precomputed or per-token, etc. Without this, it is difficult to judge whether the 40–43% speedups are representative or partly due to under-optimized dense baselines.
  - The evaluation is for a very small number of prompts (“five samples that consists of 2048 tokens,” Sec. 4.3), and only decoding is considered. There is no breakdown of time into compute vs thresholding/index selection vs data movement, and no error bars.

  As a result, while the speedups are promising, the paper does not yet establish that R-Sparse delivers robust, competitive gains in realistic deployment settings (mixed precision, batched inference, modern kernels).

- **Limited evidence for the claimed general “rank-aware structure.”**  
  The conceptual pitch is that the product of input activations and SVD components has a joint sparse structure such that a bottom-right block (large channels × large singular values) dominates (Sec. 3.3, Fig. 1/3), justifying the combined sparse/low-rank design.

  However, the empirical support is weak:
  - The importance matrix \(S_{i,j}\) and the “stable rank ≈ 400” of the bias matrix \(\mathbf{M}\) are evaluated only on Llama-2-7B, using just 16 C4 samples (Sec. 3.3). There are no quantitative plots of approximation error vs rank or vs sparsity, no fraction-of-mass curves, and no replication across models or tasks.
  - Figure 3 shows heatmaps for some layers and blocks, but both axes are independently sorted, which visually emphasizes concentration in a corner; there is no numerical threshold (e.g., “95% of mass lies in this k×k block”), and some layers appear less sharply sparse.
  - The narrative from Sec. 3.2 (“non-sparse components behave like biases approximable by a few terms”) to the final low-rank SVD-based implementation is conceptually plausible but empirically under-supported beyond a small two-task experiment (Fig. 2) and a qualitative description of the bias matrix’s rank.

  The method undeniably works reasonably well empirically, but the “rank-aware” explanation is not convincingly established as a general phenomenon; it is closer to a heuristic interpretation backed by limited analysis than a robustly demonstrated mechanism.

- **Comparative and novelty positioning are incomplete.**  
  The paper does not sufficiently situate R-Sparse relative to the broader line of sparse+low-rank compression and concurrent activation sparsity work:
  - Section 2.2 briefly mentions one concurrent method focusing solely on input channels (Liu et al., 2024a) as “a special case of our framework,” but there is no empirical or conceptual deepening of this relationship. Without experiments or more detailed discussion, it is not clear how much R-Sparse advances beyond “input activation sparsity + SVD,” particularly given that combining sparsity and low-rankness has a long history in model compression.
  - There is also no comparison or even a careful conceptual contrast with strong, widely used training-free weight sparsification/pruning approaches (e.g., SparseGPT, Wanda) that target the same memory/I/O bottleneck from a different angle. Given that R-Sparse’s biggest selling point is efficient inference under memory constraints, this omission leaves readers without a clear view of where R-Sparse sits among all practical options.
  
  This does not invalidate the method but does weaken claims of substantial originality and makes it harder for readers to assess its relative value.

### Minor

- **The multi-phase ReLU analysis (Sec. 3.2) is only loosely connected to the actual algorithm.**  
  Sec. 3.2 proposes a “multi-phase ReLU” \(\sigma_T\) and shows in Fig. 2 that for OBQA and ARC-C, moving from \(l=1\) to \(l\ge 2\) largely recovers performance even at high sparsity. It then algebraically interprets the non-sparse part as a sum of data-dependent biases \(B_j\) and suggests they can be approximated by static biases. However, R-Sparse’s final inference scheme (Sec. 3.4) does *not* use this multi-phase ReLU construction or precomputed static biases; it relies on magnitude thresholding and SVD of \(W\). Thus, while the section provides intuition, it does not concretely guide the design, and the connection should be made more explicit or toned down.

- **Search overhead and calibration dependence are underplayed.**  
  The evolutionary search for \(\rho_i\) (Sec. 3.5) is not training in the usual sense, but it is a nontrivial optimization loop that runs repeated forward passes on the full model. The paper states it “takes approximately one hour on a single A6000 GPU for Llama-2-7B,” with no timing given for Llama-3-8B or Mistral-7B. Since Table 4 shows that adaptive recipes bring up to ~1–2% accuracy improvements—especially at high sparsity—this is practically significant, and the need to re-run such a search for each model and sparsity level should be clearly acknowledged. Also, all search and importance analyses use only 16 C4 samples, and there is no study of robustness to choice/size of calibration data.

- **Threshold computation and runtime details are underspecified.**  
  In Sec. 3.4, the sparsification threshold \(t(s)\) is defined as the \(s\)-th percentile of \(|X|\), but it is unclear whether this percentile is computed per token at runtime, approximated in some way, or pre-estimated offline per layer. The cost and implementation of this quantile estimation are not described, yet in a real system this overhead could matter, especially at high throughput. Similarly, it is not fully clear whether \(\bar{X}\) in \(Y_r = (\bar{X} - \sigma_{t(s)}(X))(A_r B_r)^T\) is the full-precision input, a mean over tokens, or something else.

- **Claim of “no performance loss” is overstated.**  
  The conclusion states: “Our work demonstrates that high levels of sparsity can be achieved … without any performance loss.” This is not accurate: Table 1 and Fig. 5 show some nontrivial drops on several tasks at 50% sparsity (e.g., BoolQ, HellaSwag, some ARC variants), even if still competitive. The right framing is “small degradation at 50% for many tasks,” not “no loss.”

- **Efficiency limited to batch size implicitly equal to 1, no discussion of batched patterns.**  
  While not exactly wrong, it is an important limitation that speedups are reported only for generation with five prompts and single-sequence decoding. As many prior works on activation sparsity have discussed, per-input dynamic sparsity can be substantially less beneficial under batching, where different samples may activate disjoint sets of channels. This potential limitation is not discussed.

### Trivial

- Some minor notational ambiguities (e.g., the exact definition of “stable rank” in Sec. 3.3, the unindexed \(\bar{X}\) in Sec. 3.4) could be clarified easily.
- A few typos (“GRIFIN” vs “GRIFFIN” appears inconsistently; “ReLUfction” in Sec. 4.1 likely should be “ReLUfication”) are present but not impactful.

## Nice-to-Haves

- A visualization of the learned \(\rho_i\) across layers and blocks for at least one model, to show which layers prefer more low-rank vs more sparsity, would make the “rank-aware” narrative more concrete and provide insight into model structure.
- Sensitivity analysis of the search procedure: e.g., repeating the evolutionary search with different random seeds and different small calibration subsets to see how much \(\rho\) and downstream performance vary.
- Additional benchmarks beyond commonsense QA/short tasks—e.g., MMLU, GSM8K, or code benchmarks—to better characterize behavior on reasoning-heavy or long-horizon tasks.
- A brief comparison (empirical or at least analytical) to strong training-free weight pruning methods (SparseGPT, Wanda), focusing on memory I/O and wall-clock tradeoffs, would sharpen the practical story.

## Removed Points

These points are flagged to be removed, treat them with caution. They may reflect misunderstandings or issues outside the paper’s stated scope.

- **“Model-level sparsity does not correspond to currently available systems / cannot be independently verified.”**  
  Any language doubting the existence or release status of baselines or tools is not appropriate here. The paper cites CATS, GRIFIN, and other relevant methods; per instructions, we assume these exist and are correctly referenced.

- **Criticism that R-Sparse is not “really training-free” because it requires some hyperparameter search.**  
  While the evolutionary search is nontrivial, the paper never claims there is zero offline calibration. It correctly uses “training-free” in the sense of “no gradient updates / no continual pretraining.” The right criticism (kept above) is that the search overhead and its dependence on a small calibration set deserve more emphasis, not that the “training-free” label is inaccurate.

- **Claims that the authors misrepresent ReLUfication’s training cost as an unfounded blanket statement.**  
  The introduction’s statements (“Such training process can involve up to 150B tokens…”) are broadly consistent with cited work and not demonstrably incorrect from within the paper. Any challenge to the precise numbers would require external verification, which we cannot perform.

- **Complaints about missing specific related work names or formatting choices.**  
  We cannot reliably judge missing citations without external sources, and the user has explicitly asked to ignore formatting nitpicks.

## Novel Insights

The genuinely novel insight is that for modern non-ReLU LLMs, one can effectively exploit complementary structure in activations and weights by *jointly* leveraging input activation sparsity and rank structure in the weight matrices, and that this joint structure is sufficiently robust that a simple global magnitude threshold on inputs plus a single SVD per layer, tuned by a lightweight evolutionary search, can give practical 50% nominal sparsity with modest accuracy loss across three different model families without any retraining. This moves the activation sparsity literature beyond output-channel prediction and ReLUfication, and suggests that a significant portion of “non-sparse” activity in such models can be mediated through a low-rank pathway rather than preserved in full dense form.

## Suggestions

- **Clarify and tighten the definition and accounting of “model-level sparsity.”**  
  - Provide an explicit formula for computing model-level sparsity from layerwise \((r_i, s_i)\), ideally in units that matter (e.g., relative HBM bytes read for all weights per decoding step).  
  - For CATS and GRIFIN, re-derive their effective model-level sparsity under the *same* accounting and explain how the “scaled-up” variants are constructed.  
  - Where possible, report both “parameter I/O reduction” and “compute reduction” to make cross-method comparisons transparent.

- **Substantially strengthen the efficiency evaluation.**  
  - Include comparisons against dense FP16/BF16 baselines using vendor-optimized kernels (e.g., cuBLAS/FlashAttention), and discuss how R-Sparse fares relative to those.  
  - Provide kernel details: data layouts, how sparse and low-rank matmuls are fused, where thresholds are computed, and how intermediate results are cached.  
  - Add profiling breakdowns and variance estimates (e.g., mean ± std of token/s over more prompts and seeds).  
  - Discuss limitations in batched settings and, if feasible, provide at least one batched benchmark.

- **Make the “rank-aware” analysis more quantitative and less anecdotal.**  
  - For several representative layers and models, present curves of cumulative contribution of \(S_{i,j}\) versus area of the bottom-right block, or error vs kept region size.  
  - Define the “stable rank” metric explicitly and show its variation across layers and models.  
  - If possible, evaluate how much performance you lose *if* you just drop the top-left block in a few layers, to empirically validate the rank-aware sparsity pattern.

- **Right-size the narrative around multi-phase ReLU and biases, or connect it more directly to the method.**  
  Either (a) show how the multi-phase ReLU/bias picture concretely influenced the choice of low-rank approximation (e.g., mapping specific intervals to bias components in \(A_r B_r\)), or (b) compress Sec. 3.2 and frame it clearly as intuition rather than as a direct design basis.

- **Acknowledge and analyze the calibration/search dependence.**  
  - Explicitly note that the per-layer \(\rho\) recipe is tuned on a small C4 subset and may need to be re-run per model and sparsity budget.  
  - Explore sensitivity to calibration size (e.g., 16 vs 64 vs 256 samples) and to dataset choice (e.g., using a subset from another corpus), and report both recipe variability and downstream impact.

- **Broaden baselines and benchmarks where feasible.**  
  - Add at least a small comparison to a strong training-free weight pruning method at similar effective I/O reduction, to contextualize activation sparsity vs weight sparsity.  
  - Include one or two more challenging benchmarks (e.g., MMLU, GSM8K, or HumanEval) for at least one model, to test whether the method maintains performance on more complex reasoning or generative tasks.

- **Tone down or precisely qualify over-strong claims.**  
  Replace statements like “without any performance loss” with more honest, qualified language (e.g., “with small performance degradation on the evaluated tasks”) and make clear that speedups are demonstrated in a specific FP32 single-GPU, batch-size-1 setting.

On standard axes:  
- **Originality:** Moderate. The specific combination and input-side focus are interesting, but sparse+low-rank as a theme is well known, and concurrent input-activation methods exist.  
- **Importance:** Moderate to high. Training-free 50% sparsity with good accuracy across popular LLMs is practically valuable.  
- **Support of claims:** Mixed. Accuracy evidence is fairly strong within the evaluated regime; efficiency and “rank-aware” structural claims are under-supported.  
- **Experimental soundness:** Reasonable for accuracy; somewhat weak for efficiency and comparisons.  
- **Clarity:** Good overall.  
- **Value to the community:** Good, provided the paper is more precise about sparsity accounting and efficiency, and modest in its claims.

## Score and Decision

### Calibration

I compared this paper against several human-reviewed works:

- **TEAL (training-free activation sparsity)**: Scores mostly 8s and a 6. TEAL has a similarly ambitious training-free activation sparsity goal but provides broader evaluation and clearer sparsity accounting, and is generally regarded as strong.  
- **SVD-LLM, LASER, Double Sparse Factorization, OATS**: These compression papers with mixed or low-rank approaches commonly receive scores in the 5–8 range when they have solid experiments but some limitations in baselines or novelty.  
- **Q-Sparse, Sparsing Law, ASVD**: Rejected or borderline papers with notable experimental or conceptual shortcomings typically scored in the 3–5 range.

Compared to TEAL and the stronger accepted works, this paper is weaker in rigor around sparsity/efficiency definitions and in comparative positioning, though it has solid empirical accuracy. It is clearly stronger than Q-Sparse/Sparsing Law in experimental thoroughness and clarity.

Positioning relative to these anchors, I would place this work as **borderline but slightly below the bar**: methodologically sound enough and empirically promising, but with unresolved issues about fairness/rigor of core claims and incomplete efficiency evidence.

