1. **Interesting and intuitive evaluation framing.** The idea of "self-knowledge" — asking whether a model truly understands what it creates — is conceptually appealing and well-motivated by Feynman's principle. The "first generate, then evaluate" paradigm is easy to implement and provides a novel lens for LLM evaluation.

2. **Broad empirical coverage.** The paper evaluates 7 LLMs and 2 LMMs across 9 diverse tasks (word counting, designated word counting, facts, arXiv, math, theorem proving, code, grammar, SQL-type operations), providing a systematic overview of self-knowledge deficits across model families and task types.

3. **Multiple evaluation protocols.** The paper goes beyond the basic generate-then-evaluate setup by introducing the dual-generating strategy (Section 4.3), the content-reuse strategy (Section 4.4), and the in-context evaluation with noise (Section 6.2), which gives the framework more depth and robustness.

4. **Prompting strategy analysis.** The finding that expert-based prompts usually improve self-knowledge ability while chain-of-thought prompting usually does not is a practical and potentially useful observation.

<b>Weaknesses:</b>

1. **Insufficient depth in explaining why models fail self-knowledge tasks.** The paper primarily documents that LLMs perform poorly but provides limited insight into the root causes. The analysis of the designated word counting task (Section 6.1) is the only mechanistic attempt, but it relies on a somewhat ad-hoc "attention-based score" metric (top 15% tokens) without rigorous justification for this threshold or deeper probing of attention patterns. As noted by a reviewer of the closely related GV-consistency paper: "the paper did not provide insights or explanations or analyses on why even powerful LLMs suffer from generator-validator inconsistency" (reviewer of phBS6YpTzC.md). Similarly, the self-verification paper's reviewer noted: "The analysis of the self-critique mechanism lacks depth regarding why LLMs fail at self-critique. Specific instances of LLM outputs and their failures would enrich the discussion by pinpointing the flaws in LLM reasoning strategies" (reviewer of 4O0v4s3IzY.md). The current paper similarly lacks this depth.

2. **Conflation of "self-knowledge" with consistency/capability limitations.** The self-knowledge score conflates multiple failure modes: (a) the model fails to generate content satisfying its own prompt (e.g., generating 63 words instead of 56), (b) the model fails to correctly verify/evaluate the content, and (c) both. These are distinct issues. A model that generates the right number of words but miscounts them when asked has a different problem than one that generates the wrong number. The paper does not disentangle these failure modes. As a reviewer of the IdentityChain paper noted about self-consistency: "We could construct a perfectly self-consistent and not-so-useful CLM" — similarly, a model could get a high self-knowledge score by coincidence (e.g., generating wrong content that it also mis-evaluates to the same wrong answer) (reviewer of caW7LdAALh.md). The framework risks measuring task difficulty rather than genuine "self-knowledge."

3. **Limited novelty — poor LLM counting performance is well-established.** The total word counting and designated word counting results (near 0% for most models) are the headline findings, but it is well-known that autoregressive LLMs struggle fundamentally with token/word counting due to tokenization mismatches and architectural limitations. Several existing works (e.g., CoPE, "When Can Transformers Count to n?") have extensively studied why transformers fail at counting tasks. As a reviewer of the counting paper stated: "many of the assumptions in this paper are overly simplistic and do not align well with real-world Transformer design or training outcomes... the findings presented here have been hinted at in earlier works and are not surprising" (reviewer of WULjblaCoc.md). The paper does not adequately situate its findings relative to this existing literature on counting limitations.

4. **Fine-tuning experiments are limited and results are inconsistent.** Section 6.3 on fine-tuning with self-generated data only shows marginal improvements (e.g., Llama3 gains 3.08% on GSM-8k with correct data, GPT-3.5's improvement is near zero at 0.04% for correct data and negative for wrong data), and the paper itself acknowledges GPT-3.5's negative result as "an outlier." As a reviewer of the self-improvement paper "Mind the Gap" noted: "the main experiments are limited to one benchmark, GSM8k... it is relatively easy for current cutting-edge LLMs and its benchmarking function may decrease due to potential data contamination" (reviewer of mtJSMcF3ek.md). The self-consuming loop paper's reviewer also cautioned about limited experimental setups: "Logic expressions merely form a small part of data that LLMs can generate. Experiments with only logical expressions cannot lead to a general conclusion" (reviewer of SaOxhcDCM3.md). Similarly, fine-tuning on just the math subset of self-generated data and evaluating only on GSM-8k is too narrow to draw strong conclusions about the utility of self-knowledge frameworks for model improvement.

5. **Task selection mixes fundamentally different evaluation types, undermining the "self-knowledge" framing.** The "facts" and "ArXiv" tasks test whether the model can recall factual information it generated — this is more about hallucination and factual consistency than "self-knowledge" in the sense of understanding what one creates. The math and theorem tasks test whether the model can verify its own reasoning. The word-counting tasks test low-level perceptual/tracking ability. Lumping these all under "self-knowledge" obscures more than it reveals. As a reviewer of the logical consistency paper noted: "The evaluation sets are small, and it is not sure if the findings will be applicable in general" and "it is not clear if this connects to the concept of logical consistency" (reviewer of kJgi5ykK3t.md). The same concern applies here — the diverse tasks may not all map onto a single coherent "self-knowledge" construct.

6. **Limited number of evaluation samples and lack of variance reporting.** The paper tests only 100 samples per task and does not report standard deviations or confidence intervals. Given the binary scoring (I(a=â)), results on 100 samples could be quite noisy, and the differences between models on some tasks are small enough to be within noise margins. As a reviewer of the ARB benchmark noted about evaluation concerns: "it is still unclear... the difficulty actually came from reasoning rather than problem format or previous knowledge" (reviewer of gsZAtAdzkY.md). Without variance reporting, it is hard to assess statistical reliability of the reported differences.

